1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,192
Week seven established pregnancy physiology and maternal emergencies.
Here we use that foundation for the two obstetric sections of seven E,
especially trauma and red-flag assessment.

2
00:00:12,192 --> 00:00:24,816
The immediate task remains recognition and treatment of airway,
breathing, circulation and neurological threats. A detailed pregnancy
history follows rather than delays essential care.

3
00:00:24,816 --> 00:00:35,736
Connect each modification to a reason: less respiratory reserve,
potential hidden blood loss, uterine compression or a symptom that
needs obstetric evaluation.

4
00:00:35,736 --> 00:00:47,640
This session does not complete normal birth or neonatal resuscitation.
Those practical sequences are taught separately so maternal and
newborn decisions are not mixed into one algorithm.

5
00:00:47,640 --> 00:01:01,296
Ask the estimated gestation, how it was established and whether more
than one fetus is expected. If the information is uncertain, preserve
that uncertainty rather than turn an estimate into a confirmed date.

6
00:01:01,296 --> 00:01:14,472
Clarify pain, vaginal bleeding, fluid loss, contractions and fetal
movement when relevant. Record when each change began rather than
combine all symptoms under pregnant and unwell.

7
00:01:14,472 --> 00:01:26,664
Ask about prior deliveries, cesarean or other uterine surgery and
significant complications. The current pregnancy counts in gravida;
the number of living children is a different item.

8
00:01:26,664 --> 00:01:39,048
Ask about medicines, medical conditions, recent birth or pregnancy
loss and the specific injury mechanism. Do not assume pregnancy is
absent simply because it is not visible.

9
00:01:39,048 --> 00:01:51,192
The school describes substantial blood-volume expansion. Plasma
increases more than red-cell mass, so dilution and greater circulating
volume are related but different concepts.

10
00:01:51,192 --> 00:02:02,640
A pregnant patient can have important blood loss before a dramatic
fall in pressure. Do not use a percentage such as thirty percent as a
safe loss allowance or a prediction for an individual.

11
00:02:02,640 --> 00:02:15,528
Placental perfusion depends on maternal circulation and can be
compromised while maternal vital signs still appear compensated. A
reassuring initial pressure does not clear the fetus or the injury.

12
00:02:15,528 --> 00:02:27,888
Seek the mechanism, pain, tenderness, bleeding, perfusion and
trajectory. Treat current threats and organize appropriate definitive
assessment rather than wait for classic shock.

13
00:02:27,888 --> 00:02:40,320
Pregnancy can lower systemic vascular resistance and increase pulse
and cardiac output. These adaptations vary with gestation and do not
supply one normal value for every patient.

14
00:02:40,320 --> 00:02:52,392
Compare with a reliable individual baseline while assessing present
perfusion and symptoms. A large relative change can be important
without being a standalone diagnosis.

15
00:02:52,392 --> 00:03:05,088
Do not dismiss marked tachycardia, hypotension, chest pain, syncope or
new dyspnea as normal pregnancy. These findings require explanation in
the current clinical context.

16
00:03:05,088 --> 00:03:17,424
Conversely, the school phrase normal adult pressure may be dangerous
does not redefine pregnancy hypertension. We will keep diagnostic
thresholds separate from symptoms and relative change.

17
00:03:17,424 --> 00:03:30,648
Pregnancy increases oxygen demand while anatomical changes can reduce
the reserve available during interrupted breathing. Prepare airway and
ventilation support early when the patient is critically ill.

18
00:03:30,648 --> 00:03:42,720
Minute ventilation often rises through increased tidal volume. Do not
confuse liters per minute with breaths per minute or assume a large
new respiratory-rate increase is physiological.

19
00:03:42,720 --> 00:03:54,384
Assess breathing directly and check reliable measurements. A pulse-
oximeter value cannot establish airway protection, adequate carbon-
dioxide clearance or fetal well-being.

20
00:03:54,384 --> 00:04:06,408
Treat inadequate breathing through the appropriate authorized pathway.
Oxygen alone is not a substitute for ventilation, and a normal initial
number does not prevent later deterioration.

21
00:04:06,408 --> 00:04:15,912
The school highlights vascular and edematous airway tissues. These can
bleed with traumatic manipulation and can complicate airway access.

22
00:04:15,912 --> 00:04:29,184
Use effective basic airway maneuvers and suitable authorized
equipment, with suction and assistance ready. Gentle handling means
careful effective care, not avoiding necessary life-saving support.

23
00:04:29,184 --> 00:04:41,568
Do not choose every device one size smaller or stop all airway care
merely because bleeding appears. Reassess the problem, clear
obstruction appropriately and escalate within training and scope.

24
00:04:41,568 --> 00:04:53,256
Advanced intubation remains an appropriately trained provider's
intervention. The assessment lesson does not authorize a PCP to
perform it or delay useful bag-mask ventilation.

25
00:04:53,256 --> 00:05:09,792
Reduced lower-esophageal-sphincter tone and increased abdominal
pressure contribute to aspiration risk. Labour and opioids can slow
gastric emptying; pregnancy alone does not uniformly cause the
delayed-emptying rule stated in the school section.

26
00:05:09,792 --> 00:05:22,560
Prepare airway protection and suction according to the condition. A
routine nasogastric tube is not a guaranteed aspiration-prevention
measure or an independently authorized PCP action.

27
00:05:22,560 --> 00:05:35,064
Renal blood flow and filtration rise during pregnancy and laboratory
interpretation can change. That does not mean every medicine is
cleared faster or every dose should be increased.

28
00:05:35,064 --> 00:05:48,552
Use the relevant medicine monograph, indication, pregnancy cautions
and consultation requirements. A mechanism explains why careful
prescribing matters, not permission to invent a dose adjustment.

29
00:05:48,552 --> 00:05:59,640
The fetus depends on maternal oxygenation and circulation. Treating
maternal life threats is not a choice to ignore the fetus; it is the
immediate way to support both.

30
00:05:59,640 --> 00:06:10,656
Control external bleeding, support effective breathing and manage
perfusion through the appropriate pathway. Do not delay these tasks to
obtain a fetal heart-rate measurement.

31
00:06:10,656 --> 00:06:22,632
Add pregnancy-specific positioning and thermal protection without
losing necessary injury care. Adaptations should improve the primary
plan, not create a separate competing checklist.

32
00:06:22,632 --> 00:06:34,080
Request additional help early when maternal and newborn care or
difficult extraction may require separate teams. Pre-alert the
receiving service with the mechanism and clinical concerns.

33
00:06:34,080 --> 00:06:45,576
Describe the actual mechanism instead of only saying minor collision
or simple fall. Ask where force was applied, whether the abdomen was
compressed and when symptoms began.

34
00:06:45,576 --> 00:06:58,800
The enlarged uterus is more exposed outside the pelvis, and placental
injury may occur without a dramatic external wound. Musculoskeletal
adaptations can also change balance and injury patterns.

35
00:06:58,800 --> 00:07:13,248
Seek new abdominal pain, tenderness, contractions, bleeding or fluid
loss and movement changes. Absence of any one symptom does not
reliably clear the pregnancy after significant trauma.

36
00:07:13,248 --> 00:07:25,272
Use appropriate trauma evaluation and destination planning. A
patient's initial reassurance is valuable history but does not replace
clinical assessment or obstetric review when indicated.

37
00:07:25,272 --> 00:07:34,656
Placental abruption involves separation of the placenta from its
attachment before birth. Trauma is one context in which it must be
considered.

38
00:07:34,656 --> 00:07:49,056
Pain, uterine tenderness, contractions or vaginal bleeding may raise
concern, but the pattern varies. Do not require a rigid abdomen and a
large visible bleed before taking the problem seriously.

39
00:07:49,056 --> 00:08:00,888
Blood may be concealed, and maternal compensation can make early
pressure appear reassuring. Estimate observed loss honestly without
pretending to measure hidden internal loss.

40
00:08:00,888 --> 00:08:13,104
Field assessment cannot reliably exclude placental separation.
Continue maternal care, appropriate conveyance and early notification
rather than offer clearance from one normal finding.

41
00:08:13,104 --> 00:08:25,488
A large uterus can compress major vessels when the patient lies flat.
Reduced venous return lowers cardiac filling and can contribute to
dizziness, hypotension and impaired perfusion.

42
00:08:25,488 --> 00:08:35,880
This mechanism can coexist with hemorrhage or another cause of shock.
Improvement after repositioning does not prove there is no injury or
ongoing blood loss.

43
00:08:35,880 --> 00:08:48,984
Consider uterine relief in the appropriate later-pregnancy setting
while preserving airway and injury care. The aim is useful
circulation, not achieving a memorized tilt angle at any cost.

44
00:08:48,984 --> 00:09:00,504
Repeat symptoms, perfusion and reliable measurements after the
modification. Treat the effect as information for the whole assessment
rather than a definitive diagnostic test.

45
00:09:00,504 --> 00:09:09,840
The school uses a fundus near the umbilicus as an approximate twenty-
week landmark. It can help when the patient cannot provide a reliable
history.

46
00:09:09,840 --> 00:09:21,048
It is not a precise dating test or proof of fetal maturity, viability
or birth weight. Multiple gestation, body habitus and other factors
can alter the finding.

47
00:09:21,048 --> 00:09:33,312
Use the landmark to anticipate compression and appropriate
resuscitation modifications. Do not delay life-saving care while
repeatedly trying to establish an exact week by palpation.

48
00:09:33,312 --> 00:09:43,800
Record the reported gestation and the observed landmark as separate
information. Label their sources and uncertainty so the next team can
interpret them correctly.

49
00:09:43,800 --> 00:09:56,400
For a patient with circulation, a supported left-lateral position or
left uterine displacement can reduce compression. Choose the method
that is feasible and compatible with the clinical situation.

50
00:09:56,400 --> 00:10:09,504
In trauma, preserve indicated spinal motion restriction and secure
support while adapting position. Avoid an unstable tilted platform or
forcing a position that obstructs necessary care.

51
00:10:09,504 --> 00:10:20,184
Manual displacement is distinct from compressing or massaging the
pregnant uterus. It is a positioning intervention taught and practised
with appropriate supervision.

52
00:10:20,184 --> 00:10:30,408
Check the effect and maintain safe restraint during conveyance. Do not
assume one initial position will remain effective after every transfer
or change in condition.

53
00:10:30,408 --> 00:10:45,672
Do not transfer the non-arrest tilt rule unchanged into cardiac
arrest. Current AHA pregnancy-arrest guidance supports supine high-
quality CPR with manual left uterine displacement when the fundus is
at or above the umbilicus.

54
00:10:45,672 --> 00:10:57,240
Preserve effective compressions, defibrillation when indicated and
airway support. Positioning should reduce compression without
sacrificing the quality of the resuscitation itself.

55
00:10:57,240 --> 00:11:08,976
Request appropriate advanced and obstetric resources immediately and
communicate pregnancy size and timing. Local destination and
consultation requirements still apply.

56
00:11:08,976 --> 00:11:21,504
Resuscitative delivery is a specialist team intervention, not a new
PCP surgical permission. This lecture does not resolve every local
maternal-arrest logistics decision from a general guideline.

57
00:11:21,504 --> 00:11:32,688
Seven E repeatedly says high-flow oxygen even with a normal
saturation. Do not extend that into one rule for every stable pregnant
patient with any complaint.

58
00:11:32,688 --> 00:11:48,336
Current L ten pregnancy-trauma text specifies maternal saturation
above ninety-five percent, with airway and oxygen support as required.
Use the condition-specific pathway rather than silently replace it
with a generic target.

59
00:11:48,336 --> 00:12:02,928
A zero seven separately lists cord prolapse, shoulder dystocia and
postpartum hemorrhage among high-flow exceptions regardless of
saturation. Those specific indications are not a license to generalize
to all pregnancy.

60
00:12:02,928 --> 00:12:15,168
Never withhold required rescue support, and do not confuse a
saturation target with adequate ventilation or perfusion. Reassess the
actual patient after oxygen or airway care.

61
00:12:15,168 --> 00:12:26,736
Ask whether movement is usual and when it was last felt, taking
gestation into account. A report of reduced movement is meaningful
even without a field fetal monitor.

62
00:12:26,736 --> 00:12:41,976
A normal maternal monitor value or a report of movement does not
exclude placental injury or fetal compromise. Conversely, an inability
to feel movement early in pregnancy is not the same finding as a new
late-pregnancy reduction.

63
00:12:41,976 --> 00:12:54,000
Use fetal observations when appropriate equipment and training are
available, but never delay effective maternal resuscitation, urgent
conveyance or necessary notification to obtain them.

64
00:12:54,000 --> 00:13:04,776
Communicate the reported change and its timing without claiming a
fetal diagnosis you cannot establish. The receiving obstetric team
needs the full context.

65
00:13:04,776 --> 00:13:15,744
Current L ten includes interventions intended for several provider
levels. Read the operational level and the linked procedure, not only
the general-information paragraph.

66
00:13:15,744 --> 00:13:27,408
PCP access and fluid decisions remain indication-based and require
reassessment. Do not turn the pregnancy blood-volume expansion into a
blanket large-volume fluid prescription.

67
00:13:27,408 --> 00:13:40,920
L ten requires consultation before tranexamic acid for the relevant
hemorrhagic-shock context. Confirm the current monograph and patient
conditions rather than copy a remembered trauma dose without checking.

68
00:13:40,920 --> 00:13:54,192
Nasogastric insertion, thoracostomy, blood products and surgery are
not new PCP permissions merely because they appear on the page.
Arrange appropriately trained resources and definitive care.

69
00:13:54,192 --> 00:14:08,160
The severity of maternal injury and the need for immediate
resuscitation drive destination planning. A patient with major trauma
should not be diverted from appropriate trauma care solely because a
maternity unit exists elsewhere.

70
00:14:08,160 --> 00:14:22,368
When severity or gestation is uncertain, seek the appropriate
emergency and trauma assessment through the local pathway. Do not use
a rough fundal landmark to make an unsupported viability or hospital-
capability decision.

71
00:14:22,368 --> 00:14:36,264
Pre-alert with injury mechanism, estimated gestation, symptoms,
physiological trend and care. Include suspected obstetric
complications without converting suspicion into a confirmed diagnosis.

72
00:14:36,264 --> 00:14:47,808
Use consultation when the destination or coordination is unclear.
Continue useful care while the route is arranged and reassess whether
new deterioration changes urgency.

73
00:14:47,808 --> 00:15:00,984
The selected ISSHP framework defines pregnancy hypertension at
systolic one hundred forty or diastolic ninety or higher, with severe
range at one hundred sixty or one hundred ten or higher.

74
00:15:00,984 --> 00:15:13,128
The word or matters: both numbers do not have to cross the threshold.
Use a suitable cuff, correct technique and an appropriate repeat
rather than accept a doubtful single reading.

75
00:15:13,128 --> 00:15:24,960
Diagnostic confirmation intervals are not orders to remain on scene
for hours with severe symptoms. Assess threats, repeat promptly when
appropriate and organize urgent care.

76
00:15:24,960 --> 00:15:39,072
A relative rise below the threshold can still accompany important
illness, but it does not by itself establish preeclampsia. Symptoms
and organ involvement require evaluation rather than a new invented
cutoff.

77
00:15:39,072 --> 00:15:54,576
Preeclampsia typically involves new hypertension after twenty weeks
with qualifying maternal organ or uteroplacental dysfunction.
Proteinuria is one possible feature, not an obligatory finding in
every modern diagnostic pathway.

78
00:15:54,576 --> 00:16:05,352
Ask about severe or persistent headache, visual changes and upper-
abdominal pain. These should not automatically be dismissed as normal
pregnancy discomfort.

79
00:16:05,352 --> 00:16:19,512
New dyspnea, reduced responsiveness or evidence of organ dysfunction
increases concern. HELLP names hemolysis, elevated liver enzymes and
low platelets; it cannot be confirmed from abdominal pain alone.

80
00:16:19,512 --> 00:16:32,952
Sudden swelling can contribute to the story, but swelling alone does
not diagnose the syndrome and its absence does not exclude it. Field
recognition should lead to timely evaluation, not false certainty.

81
00:16:32,952 --> 00:16:45,984
The school lists first pregnancy, prior hypertension, kidney disease,
diabetes and multiple fetuses as risk contexts. Ask about these
without assuming that the syndrome is limited to one group.

82
00:16:45,984 --> 00:16:58,272
A previous complicated pregnancy or new concern in prenatal care is
important to communicate. Attribute the report and distinguish a
documented finding from a patient's recollection.

83
00:16:58,272 --> 00:17:08,520
No known risk factors and a normal earlier visit do not exclude
disease today. The current symptom pattern and physiological findings
still require assessment.

84
00:17:08,520 --> 00:17:18,312
Do not frame risk discussion as blame. The useful question is what the
history changes about suspicion, monitoring, urgency and
communication.

85
00:17:18,312 --> 00:17:32,304
Eclampsia involves a seizure in the relevant hypertensive pregnancy
syndrome, but the first recognized event may be the seizure itself. A
previous diagnosis of preeclampsia is not required before considering
it.

86
00:17:32,304 --> 00:17:43,560
Headache or visual symptoms may precede the event, but their absence
is not reassuring enough to exclude it. Postpartum presentation also
remains possible.

87
00:17:43,560 --> 00:17:58,992
The school wording that pregnancy seizures are most often eclampsia
should not close the differential. Hypoglycemia, epilepsy,
intracranial disease, toxicity and other causes can coexist or present
similarly.

88
00:17:58,992 --> 00:18:10,224
Treat immediate threats and follow the applicable seizure and
obstetric pathway. Do not wait for a urine test or a second classic
feature before initiating urgent support.

89
00:18:10,224 --> 00:18:21,120
Protect from injury without forcing the limbs or placing anything in
the mouth. Track seizure timing and assess breathing and
responsiveness as soon as feasible.

90
00:18:21,120 --> 00:18:32,328
Support airway and ventilation and check glucose when appropriate
without delaying essential care. Use safe positioning compatible with
pregnancy and any injury.

91
00:18:32,328 --> 00:18:44,424
Magnesium and other advanced medicines are not automatically PCP
interventions because they are discussed in the school lesson. Follow
the actual service role and consultation requirements.

92
00:18:44,424 --> 00:18:55,848
Fluid decisions also need caution in suspected preeclampsia because
pulmonary edema can occur. Do not treat every altered pregnant patient
with an automatic large bolus.

93
00:18:55,848 --> 00:19:09,840
Ask about onset, amount observed, clots, pain and associated fluid
loss or contractions. Use the patient's account and objective
observations without assuming external blood equals total loss.

94
00:19:09,840 --> 00:19:21,792
Gestation changes the possibilities, including early pregnancy loss or
ectopic disease and later placental causes. Bleeding pattern alone
does not definitively identify the source.

95
00:19:21,792 --> 00:19:36,000
Do not perform a routine internal digital vaginal examination to find
the cause or estimate dilation. Necessary authorized external
observation for imminent delivery is a different task with explanation
and privacy.

96
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:47,640
Assess shock and provide the indicated urgent plan. A small observed
amount can still accompany a serious problem, while not every spotting
episode proves catastrophic hemorrhage.

97
00:19:47,640 --> 00:20:17,424
Consider an original patient at thirty-two weeks with painless vaginal
bleeding who is becoming faint. Do not call this mild simply because
the abdomen is not painful. Begin the primary assessment and assess
perfusion while your partner arranges the urgent plan. Ask about the
bleeding and its trend, estimated gestation, previous pregnancies and
uterine surgery without delaying essential care. Do not use an
internal examination to settle the cause.

98
00:20:17,424 --> 00:20:50,664
Current L zero six treats serious late-pregnancy bleeding as an
obstetric emergency, including possible previa, abruption, rupture or
fetal-vessel bleeding. Communicate the findings and trajectory to the
appropriate receiving team early, maintain indicated support and
reassess during conveyance. Visible blood is not a reliable measure of
all blood lost. Follow the current position, treatment and
consultation requirements for your actual role; the old video is not a
medication order.

99
00:20:50,664 --> 00:21:02,592
Sudden, severe, persistent or worsening abdominal pain needs careful
assessment. Do not call it normal contractions without examining the
timeline and associated findings.

100
00:21:02,592 --> 00:21:16,152
Ask whether pain is intermittent or constant, where it began, and
whether bleeding, contractions, fluid loss, syncope or trauma is
present. Prior uterine surgery is relevant history.

101
00:21:16,152 --> 00:21:27,048
Pregnancy does not prevent appendiceal, renal, gastrointestinal,
vascular or other disease. Keep plausible alternatives visible while
treating maternal threats.

102
00:21:27,048 --> 00:21:40,368
No single pain location or field maneuver definitively confirms
uterine rupture or excludes concealed bleeding. Describe findings and
obtain timely definitive care rather than overstate certainty.

103
00:21:40,368 --> 00:21:52,008
Ask what movement is usual at this gestation and what has changed. A
new reduction after movement is established is different from not yet
perceiving movement early in pregnancy.

104
00:21:52,008 --> 00:22:05,208
Record when normal activity was last perceived and associated pain,
bleeding or trauma. Do not invent a universal count or use an
unvalidated short field observation to clear the concern.

105
00:22:05,208 --> 00:22:17,160
Maternal primary assessment remains essential. Maternal illness may
affect the fetus, while a reassuring maternal pressure or pulse does
not prove normal fetal status.

106
00:22:17,160 --> 00:22:28,104
Arrange the appropriate timely maternity evaluation and communicate
the reported change. Do not delay indicated care while trying
repeatedly to provoke movement.

107
00:22:28,104 --> 00:22:38,304
Ask about a recent birth or pregnancy loss even without a visible
pregnancy. Record the date and relevant complications, medicines and
recovery.

108
00:22:38,304 --> 00:22:49,416
Hypertensive neurological illness and serious bleeding can present
after birth. A previous uncomplicated discharge does not guarantee
that the current symptom is benign.

109
00:22:49,416 --> 00:23:01,200
New dyspnea, chest pain, syncope, fever, focal weakness or altered
state also needs a broad assessment. Avoid attributing everything to
exhaustion or normal recovery.

110
00:23:01,200 --> 00:23:12,000
Communicate postpartum status with the symptom timeline and current
findings. It changes the context without making one pregnancy-related
diagnosis inevitable.

111
00:23:12,000 --> 00:23:22,824
Introduce yourself and explain the immediate concern in plain
language. Describe what you need to assess and why before touching or
exposing a sensitive area.

112
00:23:22,824 --> 00:23:35,184
Seek appropriate consent and involve the patient in decisions whenever
possible. Pregnancy does not automatically remove decision-making
capacity or allow a partner to overrule the patient.

113
00:23:35,184 --> 00:23:45,864
Ask whether the patient wants a support person present and use
suitable language assistance. Family presence may help, but it should
not prevent private communication when needed.

114
00:23:45,864 --> 00:23:57,816
Use covering, barriers and the smallest necessary audience. Explain
unavoidable urgency without threats, judgment or a promise of an
outcome you cannot guarantee.

115
00:23:57,816 --> 00:24:09,360
Trauma can involve intimate-partner or other violence. Seek a safe
opportunity for private conversation rather than ask sensitive
questions in front of a possible source of harm.

116
00:24:09,360 --> 00:24:22,416
Use neutral questions and explain relevant confidentiality limits
accurately. Do not promise absolute secrecy, investigate beyond your
role or assume that a particular injury proves a perpetrator.

117
00:24:22,416 --> 00:24:33,024
Record what the patient says with attribution and describe objective
observations. A concern, an allegation and a confirmed fact are
different categories.

118
00:24:33,024 --> 00:24:45,048
Address immediate safety and use the appropriate consent and
safeguarding process. Preserve dignity while arranging medical care
and the resources the actual situation requires.

119
00:24:45,048 --> 00:24:55,968
This original example is separate from the school imminent-delivery
case. A patient reports an estimated twenty-nine-week pregnancy and a
low-speed restrained collision.

120
00:24:55,968 --> 00:25:08,664
They describe lower-abdominal belt pressure, new persistent pain, a
small vaginal bleed and less movement than usual. The amount of
external blood is not a measurement of internal loss.

121
00:25:08,664 --> 00:25:18,840
Do not label the event harmless because the vehicle damage looks small
or the patient walked after impact. Assess the actual mechanism and
current maternal findings.

122
00:25:18,840 --> 00:25:30,360
State concerns without announcing a confirmed placental diagnosis. The
immediate plan is maternal assessment, appropriate support and timely
trauma-obstetric coordination.

123
00:25:30,360 --> 00:25:43,152
Assume the patient is alert, with pulse one hundred four, pressure one
hundred sixteen over seventy-two and respiratory rate twenty-two. A
reliable room-air saturation is ninety-seven percent.

124
00:25:43,152 --> 00:25:54,888
There is lower-abdominal tenderness but no major external injury
found. These observations do not exclude concealed bleeding, placental
separation or an evolving problem.

125
00:25:54,888 --> 00:26:07,752
Support an appropriate compression-aware position while preserving
indicated injury care. Assess the whole patient, keep warm and obtain
relevant repeated observations without an internal examination.

126
00:26:07,752 --> 00:26:20,808
Arrange the appropriate pathway and notification. Oxygen decisions
follow current indications and the pregnancy-trauma target; this
stable saturation does not itself prove fetal well-being.

127
00:26:20,808 --> 00:26:33,672
Now assume the patient becomes pale and lightheaded, pulse rises to
one hundred twenty-four and pressure falls to ninety-two over fifty-
eight. This is a meaningful change from the initial assessment.

128
00:26:33,672 --> 00:26:46,296
Repeat primary threats and consider bleeding, compression and other
mechanisms together. Do not assume that uterine relief will
necessarily solve the problem or that hidden loss can be quantified in
the field.

129
00:26:46,296 --> 00:27:00,408
Provide indicated authorized shock care, reassess response and
escalate resources and destination planning. Any TXA or fluid decision
must meet the actual current conditions and consultation requirements.

130
00:27:00,408 --> 00:27:12,984
Update the receiving team with the measured trend, pain, bleeding and
reduced movement. Do not delay urgent conveyance for a complete fetal
assessment or a definitive field diagnosis.

131
00:27:12,984 --> 00:27:24,696
Give estimated gestation and its source, relevant prior history and
the exact mechanism. Separate the time of impact, symptom onset and
any later deterioration.

132
00:27:24,696 --> 00:27:36,456
Report pain, visible or reported blood, fluid, contractions and
movement concerns with attribution. Unknown placental or fetal status
should remain unknown.

133
00:27:36,456 --> 00:27:48,648
State maternal findings and how they changed, including reliable
oxygenation and measurement limitations. One initial reassuring set
must not conceal a later shock pattern.

134
00:27:48,648 --> 00:28:02,352
Explain positioning, airway, perfusion support and actual response,
plus consultation and resource needs. The receiving team needs enough
information to prepare appropriate trauma and obstetric care.

135
00:28:02,352 --> 00:28:11,232
You should explain why pregnancy can hide early shock and complicate
airway care without treating every abnormality as expected physiology.

136
00:28:11,232 --> 00:28:24,168
Recognize severe headache, visual change, upper pain, seizure,
bleeding, reduced movement and altered state as reasons for
appropriate urgent assessment, not as proof of one diagnosis.

137
